1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,214
A new engineer joins the ranking team.
She needs four directories out of nine

2
00:00:09,254 --> 00:00:13,844
hundred. Your CTO asks: does she have
to download and check out everything?

3
00:00:14,184 --> 00:00:19,174
Same planning meeting, second question.
Every cross-service change is five pull

4
00:00:19,214 --> 00:00:24,332
requests, five separate merge proposals,
in forty repositories that must merge in

5
00:00:24,372 --> 00:00:29,681
order. Should we move to one repository?
And a third: Google and Meta run monorepos,

6
00:00:29,721 --> 00:00:32,483
so why does git status
take seconds in ours?

7
00:00:32,823 --> 00:00:37,565
Three questions, and the answers start from
one statement: a monorepo is not a Git

8
00:00:37,605 --> 00:00:42,173
feature. It's a decision about where the
boundary of a repository lies, and Git

9
00:00:42,213 --> 00:00:46,955
stores it like any repository. Whether Git
stays fast inside that boundary depends

10
00:00:46,995 --> 00:00:50,513
on features you switch on. Hold
on to those four directories.

11
00:00:52,353 --> 00:00:56,005
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:56,045 --> 00:01:00,968
This video has two halves. First the
decision: one repository against many, as the

13
00:01:01,008 --> 00:01:05,628
textbook lists the trade-offs, and what
Git has to carry when you choose one.

14
00:01:05,668 --> 00:01:10,591
Then the first tool for living with that
choice: git sparse-checkout in cone mode,

15
00:01:10,631 --> 00:01:15,553
which writes only chosen directories into
the working tree, the folder of ordinary

16
00:01:15,593 --> 00:01:16,461
files you edit.

17
00:01:16,801 --> 00:01:22,137
The repository is orbit, a small search
platform in one repository: three services,

18
00:01:22,177 --> 00:01:27,319
two libraries, two pipelines, documentation
and tooling, 33 files, 94 commits on

19
00:01:27,359 --> 00:01:31,076
main. A commit is one saved
snapshot of the whole project.

20
00:01:31,116 --> 00:01:34,963
It's small so that you can read
every transcript to the end.

21
00:01:35,003 --> 00:01:38,784
The mechanisms are the same at
ten thousand times the size.

22
00:01:39,124 --> 00:01:46,139
One replay: labs/ch24/sparse-cone.sh. The
subcommands set, add, reapply, disable and

23
00:01:46,179 --> 00:01:52,270
init --cone are 🟡 CAUTION: they add
and remove files in the working tree.

24
00:01:52,310 --> 00:01:53,530
list is 🟢 SAFE.

25
00:01:55,370 --> 00:01:56,782
After this video you can:

26
00:01:57,122 --> 00:02:01,810
State the trade-offs of one repository
against many as the section lists them.

27
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:04,475
Say what Git has to carry in a monorepo.

28
00:02:04,815 --> 00:02:09,478
Explain a cone-mode sparse checkout in
terms of the index: what is on disk, in the

29
00:02:09,518 --> 00:02:10,511
index and in HEAD.

30
00:02:10,851 --> 00:02:12,914
Set, widen and disable a cone.

31
00:02:13,254 --> 00:02:17,126
Explain the label "experimental" that
the documentation still carries.

32
00:02:18,966 --> 00:02:25,141
The decision first. In one sentence: a
monorepo keeps many separately deployed

33
00:02:25,181 --> 00:02:30,639
projects in one repository so that
one commit can change all of them.

34
00:02:30,679 --> 00:02:36,854
A polyrepo layout gives each project its
own history, access rules and release

35
00:02:36,894 --> 00:02:37,491
cadence.

36
00:02:37,831 --> 00:02:40,618
The layouts differ in where
a change crosses a boundary.

37
00:02:40,958 --> 00:02:45,677
A change to a library and its users: in
a monorepo, one commit and one review.

38
00:02:45,717 --> 00:02:50,681
In orbit, one commit changes a schema, the
gateway and the ingest worker together.

39
00:02:50,721 --> 00:02:55,441
In a polyrepo, that's one pull request
per repository, merged in order, with a

40
00:02:55,481 --> 00:03:00,384
published version between them. Internal
dependency versions: a monorepo has one,

41
00:03:00,424 --> 00:03:04,961
what is in the tree. In a polyrepo each
consumer pins, and consumers drift.

42
00:03:05,001 --> 00:03:09,843
Access control: Git has no per-directory
read permission, so who can clone reads

43
00:03:09,883 --> 00:03:13,016
everything. In a polyrepo,
access is per repository.

44
00:03:13,356 --> 00:03:16,790
Ownership and review: a monorepo
needs path-based rules.

45
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:21,939
Continuous integration, the automated build
and tests: a monorepo must compute what

46
00:03:21,979 --> 00:03:26,964
a change affects. In a polyrepo each
repository builds itself, and integration is

47
00:03:27,004 --> 00:03:31,121
tested late. Git performance degrades
with total size unless tuned.

48
00:03:31,161 --> 00:03:36,146
Tooling: a dependency-aware build system
and its maintainers on one side, release

49
00:03:36,186 --> 00:03:41,109
and dependency-update automation on the
other. And failure radius: a broken main

50
00:03:41,149 --> 00:03:42,908
blocks everyone, or one team.

51
00:03:43,248 --> 00:03:48,253
Neither column wins. Google's paper on its
monorepo lists the benefits: one source

52
00:03:48,293 --> 00:03:52,129
of truth, shared code, atomic
changes, large-scale refactoring.

53
00:03:52,169 --> 00:03:56,497
And it lists the costs: heavy investment
in tooling and in code health.

54
00:03:56,837 --> 00:04:02,207
Now, the question about Google and Meta.
The largest monorepos are not Git.

55
00:04:02,247 --> 00:04:07,832
Google keeps its main codebase in Piper,
a centralized system it built itself.

56
00:04:07,872 --> 00:04:13,675
Meta serves its main repository from its
own server and virtual file system, with

57
00:04:13,715 --> 00:04:19,445
Sapling as the client. Microsoft did move
a very large codebase to Git: Windows,

58
00:04:19,485 --> 00:04:25,431
about 3.5 million files and 300 gigabytes
in 2017, first with a virtual file system

59
00:04:25,471 --> 00:04:29,975
and then with Scalar, whose
techniques were contributed to Git.

60
00:04:30,315 --> 00:04:33,798
The features of this video and
the next two come from that work.

61
00:04:33,838 --> 00:04:38,420
Every figure the textbook quotes for a
company is that company's own measurement and

62
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:39,136
no benchmark.

63
00:04:39,476 --> 00:04:44,337
So what does Git have to carry? Size has
independent dimensions, and each slows

64
00:04:44,377 --> 00:04:49,237
different commands. Tracked files, the
paths that have an index entry, slow git

65
00:04:49,277 --> 00:04:54,324
status, git add and git switch. Sparse-checkout
and the sparse index address that.

66
00:04:54,364 --> 00:04:58,295
Commits and trees slow path-limited
logs, blame and merge bases.

67
00:04:58,335 --> 00:05:00,280
The commit-graph addresses that.

68
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:05,488
Blobs, the stored file contents, in all
versions cost clone, fetch and disk.

69
00:05:05,528 --> 00:05:10,333
Partial clone addresses that. New objects
and packs per day slow everything

70
00:05:10,373 --> 00:05:15,435
gradually, and maintenance addresses that.
Refs, the names such as branches and

71
00:05:15,475 --> 00:05:19,827
tags, slow fetch and push. Narrow
refspecs and pruning address that.

72
00:05:19,867 --> 00:05:24,477
Sorting a complaint into the right
row is the first step of a diagnosis.

73
00:05:24,817 --> 00:05:29,468
Now sparse-checkout. In one sentence:
git sparse-checkout makes Git write only

74
00:05:29,508 --> 00:05:34,460
chosen directories into the working tree,
while the repository, its history and its

75
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:39,512
index still contain every file. The index
is Git's record of every tracked path: the

76
00:05:39,552 --> 00:05:40,775
proposed next commit.

77
00:05:41,115 --> 00:05:44,686
Now precisely. The definition
is a list of patterns in

78
00:05:44,726 --> 00:05:50,237
$GIT_DIR/info/sparse-checkout. For every
index entry the patterns don't select, Git

79
00:05:50,277 --> 00:05:55,587
sets the skip-worktree bit, a flag on that
entry. It doesn't write the file, and

80
00:05:55,627 --> 00:06:00,001
doesn't report it as deleted. You
met that bit in the index video.

81
00:06:00,341 --> 00:06:04,981
In cone mode, the default, you name
directories. Each is included with everything

82
00:06:05,021 --> 00:06:08,910
below it, and each of its parents
with the files directly inside it.

83
00:06:08,950 --> 00:06:13,764
The top level is always included. That
restriction lets Git decide inclusion by hash

84
00:06:13,804 --> 00:06:17,982
lookup instead of matching every path
against every pattern, and it's the

85
00:06:18,022 --> 00:06:22,488
precondition for the sparse index. The
older pattern mode is deprecated in the

86
00:06:22,528 --> 00:06:23,586
manual's own words.

87
00:06:23,926 --> 00:06:28,581
The label. The manual carries this
warning, in capitals: "THIS COMMAND IS

88
00:06:28,621 --> 00:06:33,790
EXPERIMENTAL. ITS BEHAVIOR, AND THE BEHAVIOR
OF OTHER COMMANDS IN THE PRESENCE OF

89
00:06:33,830 --> 00:06:37,134
SPARSE-CHECKOUTS, WILL LIKELY
CHANGE IN THE FUTURE."

90
00:06:37,174 --> 00:06:41,829
The command dates from Git 2.25, and
cone mode is the default since 2.37.

91
00:06:41,869 --> 00:06:47,231
The label is still there in 2.56. So you
use the feature knowing that details of how

92
00:06:47,271 --> 00:06:51,090
other commands behave around it
may change between versions.

93
00:06:51,430 --> 00:06:54,766
When should you not use it?
When the repository is small.

94
00:06:54,806 --> 00:06:59,740
A sparse checkout is one more piece of state
that explains why a file isn't on disk.

95
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:06,739
For the decision, the textbook's analogy is
one shared kitchen against a row of food

96
00:07:06,779 --> 00:07:11,938
stalls. In the kitchen a change to the menu
is one conversation, and one cook's mess

97
00:07:11,978 --> 00:07:17,075
slows everyone. The stalls never collide,
and agreeing on anything takes a meeting.

98
00:07:17,115 --> 00:07:20,851
The analogy breaks at cost. A
kitchen gets crowded by people.

99
00:07:20,891 --> 00:07:25,121
A repository gets slow by files,
commits and refs, whoever made them.

100
00:07:25,461 --> 00:07:30,391
For sparse-checkout: a library with closed
stacks. The catalogue lists every book,

101
00:07:30,431 --> 00:07:35,483
and your desk holds the shelves you asked
for. The analogy breaks in one place: in a

102
00:07:35,523 --> 00:07:40,271
normal clone the stacks are in your
building too, since every object is on your

103
00:07:40,311 --> 00:07:43,907
disk. Moving them out is partial
clone, two videos from now.

104
00:07:44,247 --> 00:07:49,106
The catalogue is the index. That's the
model to keep: the index lists everything,

105
00:07:49,146 --> 00:07:51,101
and the desk is the working tree.

106
00:07:52,941 --> 00:07:58,947
On the left, HEAD, the ref that says where
you are: the tree of commit 100bb99, with

107
00:07:58,987 --> 00:08:04,849
every path. In the middle, the index: 33
entries, the same paths, each marked H or

108
00:08:04,889 --> 00:08:10,391
S. S is skip-worktree. On the right, the
working tree: nine files, only the H

109
00:08:10,431 --> 00:08:10,966
entries.

110
00:08:11,306 --> 00:08:15,091
Read the caption under the left
box: every commit complete.

111
00:08:15,131 --> 00:08:19,369
A commit is made from the index,
and the index has all 33 entries.

112
00:08:19,409 --> 00:08:23,388
A commit from a sparse checkout
is a commit of the whole tree.

113
00:08:27,728 --> 00:08:32,800
Let's walk in. A full clone of orbit:
33 tracked files, listed by project.

114
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:37,891
Older tutorials start with init --cone.
The manual calls it deprecated.

115
00:08:37,931 --> 00:08:41,468
It still works, and it shows
where every cone starts.

116
00:08:41,808 --> 00:08:45,979
No directory has been named yet.
What's left in the working tree?

117
00:08:46,019 --> 00:08:47,729
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

118
00:08:52,569 --> 00:08:57,573
The top-level files, and nothing else. git
status says so in words: "You are in a

119
00:08:57,613 --> 00:09:00,750
sparse checkout with 10%
of tracked files present."

120
00:09:00,790 --> 00:09:05,483
And the working tree is clean: the
missing files aren't reported as deleted.

121
00:09:05,823 --> 00:09:11,271
Three things changed, and no object or ref
did. The two patterns read "everything

122
00:09:11,311 --> 00:09:16,760
directly at the top level, but no directory".
The switches aren't in .git/config.

123
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:22,045
They're in .git/config.worktree, which
applies to one working tree and is read

124
00:09:22,085 --> 00:09:27,669
because extensions.worktreeConfig is now
true. So each linked working tree can have

125
00:09:27,709 --> 00:09:28,550
its own cone.

126
00:09:28,890 --> 00:09:30,998
Now name the directories you work in.

127
00:09:31,338 --> 00:09:36,287
Nine files, 28 percent of the tracked
files. So the new engineer doesn't have to

128
00:09:36,327 --> 00:09:40,278
check out everything. The download
half waits for partial clone.

129
00:09:40,618 --> 00:09:46,250
Quick quiz. Nine files are on disk. How
many entries does the index have: nine,

130
00:09:46,290 --> 00:09:49,481
twenty-four or thirty-three?
Say it out loud.

131
00:09:54,321 --> 00:09:59,957
Thirty-three. Nine marked H, twenty-four
marked S. If you said nine, you counted the

132
00:09:59,997 --> 00:10:03,673
desk, not the catalogue.
Almost everyone does at first.

133
00:10:04,013 --> 00:10:10,368
Read the pattern file in pairs. /libs/
with !/libs/*/ includes libs without its

134
00:10:10,408 --> 00:10:16,520
subdirectories, as a parent. /libs/tokenizer/
includes one subdirectory with

135
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:21,053
everything below. You never
edit this file in cone mode.

136
00:10:21,393 --> 00:10:28,084
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
three boxes: HEAD, index, working tree.

137
00:10:28,124 --> 00:10:32,435
For services/gateway/app.py,
write yes or no in each.

138
00:10:32,475 --> 00:10:33,255
I'll wait.

139
00:10:38,095 --> 00:10:43,200
Yes, yes, no. The gateway is in the index,
in HEAD and in the object database.

140
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:44,321
It isn't on disk.

141
00:10:44,661 --> 00:10:48,378
The ranker's model.py, inside
the cone, is in all three.

142
00:10:48,718 --> 00:10:53,914
Only docs/runbooks was requested.
Will docs/architecture.md appear?

143
00:10:53,954 --> 00:10:55,555
Make your prediction.

144
00:11:00,395 --> 00:11:04,440
It does. docs became a parent, and
parents bring their own files.

145
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:06,136
There's no switch for that.

146
00:11:06,476 --> 00:11:10,214
disable writes every file back
and sets the switches to false.

147
00:11:10,254 --> 00:11:12,590
The pattern file stays on disk, unused.

148
00:11:26,430 --> 00:11:28,078
Five mistakes to watch for.

149
00:11:28,418 --> 00:11:32,308
Believing a commit from a sparse
checkout contains only the cone.

150
00:11:32,348 --> 00:11:37,206
Root cause: the index still has every
entry, and a commit is made from the index.

151
00:11:37,546 --> 00:11:40,589
Expecting a teammate to get
your cone after a pull.

152
00:11:40,629 --> 00:11:45,667
Root cause: a sparse checkout is a property
of one working tree; it is not committed

153
00:11:45,707 --> 00:11:46,574
and not pushed.

154
00:11:46,914 --> 00:11:50,556
Editing .git/info/sparse-checkout
by hand in cone mode.

155
00:11:50,596 --> 00:11:55,913
Root cause: cone mode generates parent
and recursive pattern pairs; the commands

156
00:11:55,953 --> 00:11:56,851
maintain them.

157
00:11:57,191 --> 00:12:00,627
Being surprised by files next
to the requested directory.

158
00:12:00,667 --> 00:12:05,505
Root cause: every parent of a cone directory
is included with the files directly

159
00:12:05,545 --> 00:12:06,115
inside it.

160
00:12:06,455 --> 00:12:10,657
Moving to a monorepo to restrict who
reads what. Root cause: Git has no

161
00:12:10,697 --> 00:12:14,361
per-directory read permission;
who can clone reads everything.

162
00:12:16,201 --> 00:12:21,179
Now, out of the lab. An AI and ML platform
team decides its layout by coupling, as

163
00:12:21,219 --> 00:12:26,258
the textbook suggests. Feature code, the
training pipeline, serving code and shared

164
00:12:26,298 --> 00:12:29,685
schemas change together and
benefit from one repository.

165
00:12:29,725 --> 00:12:34,397
Components with other owners or other
access rules are separate repositories,

166
00:12:34,437 --> 00:12:36,538
because Git can't hide a directory.

167
00:12:36,878 --> 00:12:41,783
Model weights and datasets belong in neither.
The textbook adds a caveat that I'll

168
00:12:41,823 --> 00:12:46,788
repeat: no primary study of this choice for
ML platforms was found for this course.

169
00:12:46,828 --> 00:12:48,959
Treat it as a trade-off, not a rule.

170
00:12:49,299 --> 00:12:54,240
In the shared repository, the team wants
every new member of the ranking group to

171
00:12:54,280 --> 00:12:58,791
start with the same cone. Because a
cone isn't committed, they put the git

172
00:12:58,831 --> 00:13:01,988
sparse-checkout set line
into the onboarding script.

173
00:13:02,028 --> 00:13:07,031
For scale, the textbook cites Canva, which
used the feature to leave out generated

174
00:13:07,071 --> 00:13:11,828
translation files, about 70 percent of
all files in its repository, by its own

175
00:13:11,868 --> 00:13:12,320
account.

176
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:18,217
Your turn. Do Lab 18.2,
"Cone-mode sparse-checkout", in

177
00:13:18,257 --> 00:13:24,399
lab-manual/m18-transfer-scale.md. Before
each set or add, write down three numbers:

178
00:13:24,439 --> 00:13:28,421
files on disk, entries in
the index, entries marked S.

179
00:13:28,461 --> 00:13:30,805
Then check with git ls-files -t.

180
00:13:31,145 --> 00:13:36,988
The challenge is Exercise 18.8,
"A file that is not on disk", in

181
00:13:37,028 --> 00:13:39,838
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

182
00:13:41,678 --> 00:13:44,928
Question 423 of the CTO question bank:

183
00:13:47,268 --> 00:13:51,635
Answer out loud. A strong answer takes the
three places one at a time and gives a

184
00:13:51,675 --> 00:13:56,096
count or a rule for each. It names the bit
that makes the difference and says what

185
00:13:56,136 --> 00:14:00,448
that bit tells Git to do and not to do.
It adds what cone mode restricts and why

186
00:14:00,488 --> 00:14:04,039
that restriction exists, and it
draws the consequence for commits.

187
00:14:05,879 --> 00:14:06,806
Let's land this.

188
00:14:07,146 --> 00:14:08,806
You should now be able to say:

189
00:14:09,146 --> 00:14:14,047
A monorepo is a decision about a repository
boundary; it trades coordination across

190
00:14:14,087 --> 00:14:16,191
repositories for tooling inside one.

191
00:14:16,531 --> 00:14:19,843
Size has independent dimensions,
and each has its own remedy.

192
00:14:20,183 --> 00:14:25,376
In a sparse checkout, HEAD and the index
describe every file; the working tree holds

193
00:14:25,416 --> 00:14:28,055
only entries without
the skip-worktree bit.

194
00:14:28,395 --> 00:14:32,819
Cone mode selects directories, with their
parents' own files and the top level.

195
00:14:33,159 --> 00:14:36,914
The cone is local to one working tree,
and the command is still labelled

196
00:14:36,954 --> 00:14:38,337
experimental in the manual.

197
00:14:40,177 --> 00:14:48,642
Read sections 24.1 to 24.4 of Chapter
24. Do Exercise 18.3, "Cone-mode sparse

198
00:14:48,682 --> 00:14:53,613
checkout", in
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

199
00:14:53,953 --> 00:14:58,347
Today you counted it yourself: nine
files on the desk, thirty-three in the

200
00:14:58,387 --> 00:15:03,200
catalogue. Practise that count in the lab.
Next time we live in a sparse checkout

201
00:15:03,240 --> 00:15:07,934
and meet the sparse index. Until then,
look at the state first and type second.

202
00:15:07,974 --> 00:15:09,372
See you in the next one.
